WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
VOICE OVER: Exoskeletons - devices&nbsp;
designed to augment and enhance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.605
mobility are fast becoming a reality.

00:00:07.605 --> 00:00:11.520
PATRICK SLADE: An exoskeleton is really&nbsp;
beneficial because it allows us to improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.500
certain aspects of our mobility. So for&nbsp;
example if we want to walk with less effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.500
or walk more quickly, or overcome uh perhaps&nbsp;
mobility disorder which limits our movements.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.620
VO: But there are challenges&nbsp;
to overcome if they are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.800
to leave the lab and enter our everyday lives.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.420
PATRICK: It's actually quite difficult to&nbsp;
make walking easier or to reduce knee pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.700
or to do these things that we care about because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.080
when we walk around every day we walk in&nbsp;
short bouts of walking, we change speeds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.920
we're not walking at a on a treadmill&nbsp;
at a fixed speed like we do in the lab.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.940
VO: Now, researchers think&nbsp;
they’ve made a big step forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.380
with a smart exoskeleton&nbsp;
that can learn, as you walk.

00:00:56.185 --> 00:01:01.740
PATRICK: So what we've built is an ankle&nbsp;
exoskeleton. So this is as you can see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.960
shoe with a motorised unit, sort of like&nbsp;
a motorised boot and it applies assistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.280
as you're walking, so as you walk it&nbsp;
can apply assistance with push off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.460
and this can both make walking easier, so&nbsp;
require less effort and increase walking speed.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:24.000
VO: To provide the most benefit, exoskeletons&nbsp;
need to be carefully personalised to each user.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.500
PATRICK: In the past we've done this in the&nbsp;
lab using a big machine which measures your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.180
breathing your oxygen and your carbon dioxide,&nbsp;
to understand your energy expenditure and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.300
this huge lab-based equipment and it&nbsp;
takes hours to run these experiments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.620
so it's not really feasible uh to do on&nbsp;
devices that you might purchase and wear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.160
and so one really cool part of&nbsp;
this project is we figured out how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.400
to actually personalise assistance for each&nbsp;
person as they're walking in everyday life.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.500
VO: When a user first puts on the device,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.280
the exoskeleton will start&nbsp;
to monitor the way they move.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.160
PATRICK: So as they're walking in&nbsp;
short bouts, starting, stopping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.260
and also changing speed the device&nbsp;
is able to collect data based on how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.220
they're moving and figure out what's&nbsp;
the best way to assist each person.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.180
VO: There are various ways that the&nbsp;
exoskeleton can tweak its support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:17.820
from the timing of a boost to a step, to&nbsp;
the torque or angle of the assistance.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:23.040
But to work out which intervention&nbsp;
may be most helpful - it runs a model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.420
trained on lab data, which&nbsp;
predicts metabolic benefits.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.640
This then allows the exoskeleton to try&nbsp;
different forms of assistance - ranking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.480
and comparing options until it’s&nbsp;
optimised for the best results.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.240
PATRICK: And this doubles the benefits compared&nbsp;
to a generic non-personalised controller.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.340
VO: And because the system is designed to&nbsp;
work in the real world, Patrick and his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:53.520
wanted to really put it through its paces. So&nbsp;
they sent subjects out onto their university&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.622
campus wearing the exoskeleton and prompted&nbsp;
them to walk in a variety of different ways…

00:02:59.622 --> 00:03:01.774
EXPERIMENT AUDIO: Walk as if you're walking&nbsp;
across the street.

00:03:01.774 --> 00:03:04.560
Walk as if you're walking home with a group of friends at night.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.080
Walk as&nbsp;if you're walking home after a really bad day.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:14.700
VO: Participants walked around 9% faster, and used&nbsp;
17% less energy while wearing the boots compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:21.420
to normal shoes. Patrick says this optimisation&nbsp;
technique could have an impact across robotics

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:27.060
PATRICK: The really exciting science&nbsp;
takeaway is that we've developed a method for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:32.880
robots to optimise, as humans wear them&nbsp;
during everyday life, and so this can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.440
this idea and these approaches can be broadly&nbsp;
applied to many different kinds of robotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.880
devices beyond just exoskeletons, where you&nbsp;
have a human and a robot working together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:47.640
and so this could be an exciting uh chance&nbsp;
to see developments in for example human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.440
robot worker collaboration or other&nbsp;
aspects of sort of smart home systems.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.460
VO: And he hopes that further&nbsp;
research will allow the ankle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.220
exoskeleton to help those who need it most.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:05.220
PATRICK: We've never seen a device that you&nbsp;
can put on and take outside and move how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.000
normally do, starting, stopping and changing&nbsp;
speeds, and actually see mobility benefits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.260
and so that's really exciting because in the&nbsp;
lab these devices have been very promising and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:18.120
there's been decades of research, and now we're&nbsp;
seeing that these can be translated to perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.740
commercial products that you can buy and wear&nbsp;
and you know improve your mobility and be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.580
to do things that you otherwise couldn't&nbsp;
do. We're excited and starting to study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.620
you know how we can apply these same ideas&nbsp;
and devices to older adults for example to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.840
help make walking easier and increase speed of&nbsp;
walking. And how specific populations who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.540
other challenges beyond just walking effort and&nbsp;
walking speed for example knee pain, we can try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:48.480
to improve other metrics using these same sorts&nbsp;
of personalisations approaches in the real world.

